feat(touch): power both rails at bring-up, clock the SE, report the identity

The S3908 on SE13 is unpowered on every reboot: both supplies are RPMh-owned
VREG registers that come back 0x00, and an unpowered target aborts the
transfer with GENI state 0x21 instead of NACKing.  0130 proved the TCS path
by hand; this makes the bring-up take it.

  - kebab_touch_power=1 (default off) makes kebab_touch_start() read each
    enable through the same SPMI observer path PWR? uses, skip a rail that
    already reads 0x80, send one ENABLE for ldoa13 and then one for ldoc1
    through kebab_spmi_vrm_enable() -- the console path 0130 was measured
    through, so the encoder, the whitelist, the per-rail one-shot gate and
    the dead-channel rule are the ones already on hardware -- wait for each
    TCS to complete, and read the rail back.  One line per rail carries the
    state-machine outcome, CMD_STATUS and the enable before and after.
  - the sequence still requires kebab_vrm_write=1: kebab_touch_power is a
    second gate on the same writes, not a replacement for the safety one.
  - 15 ms after the second rail and 20 ms after the reset release, both
    inside the 10-20 ms the hardware was measured in.
  - failure is soft: the driver prints which rail failed and why, marks
    touch unavailable and returns -1.  kebab_touch_start() becomes int for
    that and bsd_init ignores the value; nothing panics, loops or retries,
    and a failed command already closes the channel for the boot.
  - the boot path now enables the SE13 branch clock before it touches GENI.
    That is not cosmetic: 0116 measured that the first GENI register group
    written without the clock stops the console, and this bring-up stopped
    at the same line ("se_init: force default, output ctrl") until TOUCHCLK
    was moved into the boot path, which is the step TOUCHSE has always done
    from the console.  With it, se_init completes and the identify command
    reaches 0x4b.
  - identify prints the payload as raw bytes before decoding it, and then
    locates the part number instead of indexing a fixed offset: the part
    string is the only self-evident field, and version/mode are the two
    bytes in front of it in the vendor struct.  Anything in front of those
    is printed as an uninterpreted prefix.  A message of the wrong kind is
    now read out of the stream instead of being decoded as the answer to
    the next command.
  - boot info names the answer: code 0x0e on this firmware is
    "not implemented", which is what an application-mode firmware does.
  - a chip that is not in application mode is reported as such and the
    driver stops.  The old code sent CMD_RUN_APPLICATION_FIRMWARE and
    re-identified, which assumed a mode switch this driver never verified;
    making the touch firmware run needs a firmware download, which stays
    out of scope.  When the report program is not accepted the poll thread
    is not started either, because polling would only print a framing error
    every interval on the only readable channel this port has.

Measured on hardware (boot_b, kebab_touch=1 kebab_touch_boot=1
kebab_touch_power=1 kebab_vrm_write=1 kebab_pon_write=1): ldoa13 EN 0x00 ->
0x80 and ldoc1 EN 0x00 -> 0x80, each with phase COMPLETE and CMD_STATUS
0x00010101; TOUCHCLK moves 0x52008 from 0x063c83c0 to 0x0e3c83c0; the reset
pulse runs; identify answers message 0x10 with 24 bytes

  a5 03 01 01 73 33 39 30 38 2d 31 35 2e 30 2e 30 00 00 00 00 b9 ba 3c 00

which is part number "s3908-15.0.0", version 0x01, mode 0x01 -- application
mode, so the touch firmware is already running and no download is needed.
get-boot-info answers 0x0e (not implemented) with no payload.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c b/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c
--- a/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c
@@ -1357,6 +1357,133 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* -------------------------------------------------------- boot rail power */
+
+#define KEBAB_VRM_EN_ON 0x80u
+
+/*
+ * The enable byte PWR? prints, split out so the boot sequence can ask whether a
+ * rail is already on before commanding it.  Same observer reads, same two
+ * matching reads of the same register: this is the path the console has
+ * already been measured through, not a second one.
+ */
+static const struct kebab_spmi_power *
+kebab_spmi_rail_target(const char *peripheral)
+{
+	uint32_t i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < sizeof(kebab_spmi_power_targets) /
+	    sizeof(kebab_spmi_power_targets[0]); i++) {
+		if (strcmp(kebab_spmi_power_targets[i].name, peripheral) == 0) {
+			return &kebab_spmi_power_targets[i];
+		}
+	}
+	return NULL;
+}
+
+static int
+kebab_spmi_rail_enable_read(const char *rail, const char *peripheral,
+    uint8_t *out)
+{
+	const struct kebab_spmi_power *t = kebab_spmi_rail_target(peripheral);
+	struct kebab_spmi_match matches[8];
+	uint32_t ppid;
+
+	if (t == NULL) {
+		printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s is not a peripheral this file knows\n",
+		    rail);
+		return -1;
+	}
+	ppid = (t->sid << 8) | (t->base >> 8);
+	if (kebab_spmi_scan(ppid, matches, 8) == 0) {
+		printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s: no APID maps PPID 0x%03x\n",
+		    rail, ppid);
+		return -1;
+	}
+	return kebab_spmi_power_read(matches[0].apid, t->sid,
+	    t->base + 0x46u, "enable", out);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The two-rail power sequence for a boot that asked for it with
+ * kebab_touch_power=1.  It is the VROMON sequence with the console removed:
+ * read the enable, encode and send one ENABLE, wait for the TCS to complete,
+ * read the enable back, then the same for the second rail.  It calls
+ * kebab_spmi_vrm_enable() instead of re-implementing the send, so the encoder,
+ * the RPMh whitelist, the per-rail one-shot gate and the dead-channel rule are
+ * exactly the ones VROMON was measured through.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 only when both rails end at 0x80.  Any failure returns -1 and
+ * leaves the channel closed for the boot; there is no retry here, because a
+ * second attempt into a state machine that refused the first one is exactly
+ * what that gate exists to prevent.  The caller must not retry either.
+ */
+int
+kebab_spmi_vrm_boot_power(void)
+{
+	static const struct {
+		const char *rail;
+		const char *peripheral;
+	} order[2] = {
+		{ "ldoa13", "pm8150_l13" },
+		{ "ldoc1",  "pm8150a_l1"  },
+	};
+	uint32_t i;
+
+	printf("KEBAB VRM: === boot power: touch rails, one ENABLE each ===\n");
+	if (!kebab_spmi_vrm_writes_enabled()) {
+		printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: kebab_vrm_write=0, so no rail may be commanded; touch stays unpowered\n");
+		return -1;
+	}
+	for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
+		uint8_t before = 0;
+		uint8_t after = 0;
+		int rc;
+
+		if (kebab_spmi_rail_enable_read(order[i].rail,
+		    order[i].peripheral, &before) != 0) {
+			printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s enable unreadable; nothing sent\n",
+			    order[i].rail);
+			return -1;
+		}
+		if (before == KEBAB_VRM_EN_ON) {
+			printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s already on (EN 0x%02x), skipped\n",
+			    order[i].rail, before);
+			/*
+			 * ldoc1 is only allowed to follow a rail that is up.  A
+			 * skipped ldoa13 is up, so record that without claiming
+			 * a send: ldoa13_done stays false, so a later VROMON may
+			 * still spend the one-shot on it.
+			 */
+			if (i == 0) {
+				vrm.ldoa13_ok = true;
+			}
+			continue;
+		}
+		rc = kebab_spmi_vrm_enable(order[i].rail);
+		if (kebab_spmi_rail_enable_read(order[i].rail,
+		    order[i].peripheral, &after) != 0) {
+			printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s: the read-back failed; the rail must not be assumed on\n",
+			    order[i].rail);
+			return -1;
+		}
+		printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s %s, phase %s, CMD_STATUS 0x%08x (ISSUED %u, COMPL %u), EN 0x%02x -> 0x%02x\n",
+		    order[i].rail, rc == 0 ? "COMPL" : "ERROR",
+		    kebab_vrm_phase_name(vrm.channel.phase),
+		    vrm.channel.last_status,
+		    (vrm.channel.last_status & KEBAB_TCS_CMD_ISSUED) != 0,
+		    (vrm.channel.last_status & KEBAB_TCS_CMD_COMPL) != 0,
+		    before, after);
+		if (rc != 0 || after != KEBAB_VRM_EN_ON) {
+			printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: %s is not up; stopping here, the boot continues without touch\n",
+			    order[i].rail);
+			return -1;
+		}
+	}
+	printf("KEBAB VRM: boot power: ldoa13 and ldoc1 both read 0x80\n");
+	return 0;
+}
+
 void
 kebab_rpmh_report(void)
 {
